+# Dynamic columns (a key/value store of extra columns)
+
+> A single entity property of type `IDictionary<string, object?>` can act as a **dynamic-columns store**: on read the declared physical columns are added to the `SELECT` projection and their values are placed into the dictionary under those names, and on write the dictionary's keys are rendered as quoted table columns. Declare the store with [`DynamicColumnsAttribute`](xref:NextORM.Core.DynamicColumnsAttribute) or fluently with [`EntityPropertyBuilder<T>.DynamicColumns(...)`](xref:NextORM.Core.EntityPropertyBuilder`1.DynamicColumns(System.String[])).
+
+**Prerequisites:** [Entities and metadata](../getting-started/03-entities-and-metadata.md) · [Querying and projections](../querying/index.md) · [Data modification (INSERT)](17-insert-statement.md) · [Data modification (UPDATE)](19-update-statement.md) · [Limitations](../advanced/limitations.md)
+
+A dynamic-columns store lets one entity shape carry columns that are not modelled as CLR properties - for example tenant-defined or schema-migrated columns. The store is a plain `Dictionary<string, object?>` (any type assignable from `IDictionary<string, object?>`) whose keys are column names and whose values are the provider's `object?` values.
+
+## Storage model
+
+| Provider | How the store travels |
+|---|---|
+| SQL Server | dictionary values in the declared columns, read back as `object?` |
+| PostgreSQL | dictionary values in the declared columns, read back as `object?` |
+| MySQL / MariaDB | dictionary values in the declared columns, read back as `object?` |
+| SQLite | dictionary values in the declared columns, read back as `object?` |
+| ClickHouse | dictionary values in the declared columns, read back as `object?` |
+| In-memory | the `Dictionary<string, object?>` value itself; queries return the entity object as-is |
+
+Values are the provider's `object?` values - no per-column CLR typing or value-converter is applied to the store. On read a database `DBNull` is placed into the store as `null`. In-memory queries return the entity object unchanged, so the store dictionary travels with it.
+
+## Declaring the store
+
+Use the attribute on the property ([`DynamicColumnsAttribute`](xref:NextORM.Core.DynamicColumnsAttribute)) with the physical columns to drain into the store:
+
+```csharp
+[SqlTable("orders")]
+public class Order
+{
+    public int Id { get; set; }
+
+    // A dictionary keyed by the physical column name.
+    [DynamicColumns("shipped_at", "tracking_no")]
+    public Dictionary<string, object?> Extras { get; set; } = new();
+}
+```
+
+With no names, `[DynamicColumns]` declares a store with an empty column set:
+
+```csharp
+[DynamicColumns]
+public Dictionary<string, object?> Extras { get; set; } = new();
+```
+
+The fluent equivalent is [`EntityPropertyBuilder<T>.DynamicColumns(params string[])`](xref:NextORM.Core.EntityPropertyBuilder`1.DynamicColumns(System.String[])):
+
+```csharp
+ctx.From<Order>(b => b
+    .Table("orders")
+    .Property(x => x.Extras).DynamicColumns("shipped_at", "tracking_no"));
+```
+
+An entity may declare **exactly one** store, its type must be assignable from `IDictionary<string, object?>`, and the property maps no single column, so it cannot be combined with `HasColumnName`, `Key`, `Identity`, `Computed`, a value converter, `JsonColumn` or `RangeColumns`.
+
+## Read semantics
+
+On read the declared dynamic columns are added to the projection as physical columns quoted by the dialect, and the row's values are placed into the store under those names. A `DBNull` becomes `null`; every value is exposed as the provider's `object?`:
+
+```csharp
+var order = ctx.From<Order>().Where(x => x.Id == 1).Single();
+
+object? shippedAt = order.Extras["shipped_at"];   // provider object? value, or null
+object? tracking  = order.Extras["tracking_no"];
+```
+
+The declared columns are selected alongside the mapped ones (SQLite output; each provider quotes identifiers with its own delimiter and uses its own parameter markers):
+
+```sql
+select id, "shipped_at", "tracking_no" from orders where id = 1;
+```
+
+Only the **declared** columns are projected; with an empty declared list no extra columns are projected. Discovery of a table's extra columns from the database schema at query-preparation time is not implemented yet - see [Limitations](#limitations) - so declare every column you want to read.
+
+## Write semantics
+
+Single-entity `INSERT` (`.Values(entity)`), `UPDATE` (`.Set(entity)`) and `MERGE` (`.Using(...)`) render the store dictionary's keys as quoted table columns. Bulk insert (`BulkInsertInto`) and the projection/mapping overloads (`Values(source, mapping)`, `Value(...)`) do **not** write the store.
+
+With a declared list, **exactly those columns** are written, in the declared order. A key missing from the dictionary becomes SQL `DEFAULT` on INSERT/MERGE and is skipped on UPDATE. An inserted row where `tracking_no` is absent renders:
+
+```csharp
+var order = new Order
+{
+    Id = 7,
+    Extras = new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["shipped_at"] = DateTime.UtcNow },
+    // "tracking_no" omitted -> DEFAULT
+};
+
+ctx.InsertInto<Order>().Values(order).Insert();
+```
+
+```sql
+-- SQLite output; a declared-but-missing key becomes DEFAULT
+insert into orders (id, "shipped_at", "tracking_no") values ($p0, $p1, default);
+```
+
+An UPDATE writes only the keys the store actually holds and skips the missing ones:
+
+```csharp
+var patch = new Order
+{
+    Id = 7,
+    Extras = new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["tracking_no"] = "1Z-999" },
+};
+
+ctx.Update<Order>().Set(patch).Where(x => x.Id == 7).Update();
+```
+
+```sql
+-- "shipped_at" is not in the store, so it is not in the SET list
+update orders set "tracking_no" = $p0 where id = $p1;
+```
+
+With **no declared list**, only the store keys that are not mapped columns are written. For a multi-row `Values(entities)` or `MERGE` the union of those keys across the written rows is rendered; a row that lacks a key gets `DEFAULT` for it (INSERT/MERGE), and an UPDATE skips it. A store key that collides with a mapped column of the entity (for example `Id`) is ignored, so a hostile value cannot overwrite a mapped column:
+
+```csharp
+var orders = new[]
+{
+    new Order { Id = 8, Extras = new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["shipped_at"] = DateTime.UtcNow } },
+    new Order { Id = 9, Extras = new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["tracking_no"] = "1Z-100" } },
+};
+
+ctx.InsertInto<Order>().Values(orders).Insert();
+// the two keys are unioned: insert into orders (id, "shipped_at", "tracking_no") ...
+```
+
+## Keys, quoting and injection safety
+
+Every store key and every declared name must be a plain SQL identifier matching `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`. Identifiers are always quoted through the dialect's delimiter (double quotes, brackets or backticks, with an embedded delimiter doubled), so a store value can never be interpolated as SQL - the feature is injection-safe. A name that does not match the identifier form throws `InvalidOperationException`, both when the metadata is built and when a dictionary key is rendered.
+
+```csharp
+[DynamicColumns("bad-name")]                     // throws InvalidOperationException
+public Dictionary<string, object?> Extras { get; set; } = new();
+
+var extras = new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["x); drop table orders; --"] = 1 };
+// rendering this store throws InvalidOperationException; the text never reaches the SQL
+```
+
+## Query plan cache
+
+The declared column set participates in the query plan key, so two stores with different declared sets produce different plans. Keep a store's declaration stable: changing the names changes the plan and adds a new cache entry rather than reusing the previous one. See [Query reuse](../guide/13-query-reuse.md).
+
+## In-memory
+
+The in-memory provider has no columns to render. A query returns the entity object as-is and the store dictionary travels with it, so the same entity and store work unchanged against an in-memory [`IDataContext`](xref:NextORM.Core.IDataContext).
+
+## Limitations
+
+* **Automatic discovery of the table's extra columns from the database schema at query-preparation time is not implemented yet (deferred).** Only explicitly declared columns are read or written; with an empty declaration no extra columns are projected on read. Declare the columns you need. This is the main gap to keep in mind when a column is added to the table after the mapping is written.
+* **One store per entity.** Declaring a second `[DynamicColumns]` property (or two fluent stores) throws `InvalidOperationException`. Declared names must be unique and must not duplicate a mapped column; a duplicate throws `InvalidOperationException` at metadata build.
+* **Public parameterless constructor required.** When an entity declares a store the materializer fills it through member-init, so the entity type must have a public parameterless constructor.
+* **In-memory non-identity projections.** The identity in-memory read (`From<T>()`) carries the store with the entity. A non-identity in-memory projection of an entity that declares a store is not supported.
+* **Store keys that collide with mapped columns.** On write, a runtime store key that equals a mapped column of the entity (compared case-insensitively) is silently ignored; a declared allow-list entry that collides is rejected at metadata build.
+* **Dictionary shape is fixed.** The property must be assignable from `IDictionary<string, object?>`; a differently keyed or valued dictionary is rejected when the metadata is built.
+* **No typed conversion.** Store values are the provider's `object?` values with no value converter, enum mapping or JSON handling; convert them at the call site if needed.
+* **No single-column mapping.** A dynamic-columns store cannot be combined with `HasColumnName`, `Key`, `Identity`, `Computed`, a value converter, `JsonColumn` or `RangeColumns`, and it is never itself a projected column.
+* **Inexact identifier set.** The allow-list is the identifier syntax, not the catalog; a declared name that does not exist in the table fails at execution time, like any other unknown column.
+
+## See also
+
+* [Entities and metadata](../getting-started/03-entities-and-metadata.md) for attribute and fluent mapping.
+* [Value converters and JSON columns](28-value-converters.md) when a column needs typed conversion instead.
+* [API reference - mapping attributes](../advanced/api-reference.md).
